{"id": 9, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَسُعُرٍ", "translit": "ٍrُʿعُsَw", "root": "سعر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "أَءُلْقِىَ ٱلذِّكْرُ عَلَيْهِ مِنۢ بَيْنِنَا بَلْ هُوَ كَذَّابٌ أَشِرٌ ", "translation": "Has the message been sent down upon him from among us? Rather, he is an insolent liar.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَءُلْقِىَ", "translit": "َàِqْlُءَaʾ", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلذِّكْرُ", "translit": "ُrْkِّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِ", "translit": "ِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِنۢ", "translit": "ۢnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنِنَا", "translit": "āَnِnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَلْ", "translit": "ْlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هُوَ", "translit": "َwُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "كَذَّابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَشِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِhsَaʾ", "root": "اشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "سَيَعْلَمُونَ غَدًا مَّنِ ٱلْكَذَّابُ ٱلْأَشِرُ ", "translation": "They will know tomorrow who is the insolent liar.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيَعْلَمُونَ", "translit": "َnwُmَlْʿعَyَs", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "غَدًا", "translit": "āًdَغ", "root": "غدو"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَّنِ", "translit": "ِnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْكَذَّابُ", "translit": "ُbāَّhdَkْlٱ", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَشِرُ", "translit": "ُrِhsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "اشر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "إِنَّا مُرْسِلُوا۟ ٱلنَّاقَةِ فِتْنَةً لَّهُمْ فَٱرْتَقِبْهُمْ وَٱصْطَبِرْ ", "translation": "Indeed, We are sending the she-camel as trial for them, so watch them and be patient.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مُرْسِلُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُlِsْrُm", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلنَّاقَةِ", "translit": "ِẗَqāَّnlٱ", "root": "نوق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِتْنَةً", "translit": "ًẗَnْtِf", "root": "فتن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَّهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَٱرْتَقِبْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْbِqَtْrٱَf", "root": "رقب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "وَٱصْطَبِرْ", "translit": "ْrِbَṭْṣٱَw", "root": "صبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "وَنَبِّئْهُمْ أَنَّ ٱلْمَآءَ قِسْمَةٌۢ بَيْنَهُمْۖ كُلُّ شِرْبٍ مُّحْتَضَرٌ ", "translation": "And inform them that the water is shared between them,[1] each [day of] drink attended [by turn].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنَبِّئْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْyʾِّbَnَw", "root": "نبا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنَّ", "translit": "َّnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْمَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَmْlٱ", "root": "موه"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "قِسْمَةٌۢ", "translit": "ٌۢẗَmْsِq", "root": "قسم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "شِرْبٍ", "translit": "ٍbْrِhs", "root": "شرب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مُّحْتَضَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَḍَtْḥُّm", "root": "حضر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "فَنَادَوْا۟ صَاحِبَهُمْ فَتَعَاطَىٰ فَعَقَرَ ", "translation": "But they called their companion,[1] and he dared[2] and hamstrung [her].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَنَادَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَdāَnَf", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَاحِبَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَbِḥāَṣ", "root": "صحب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَتَعَاطَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَṭāَʿعَtَf", "root": "عطو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَعَقَرَ", "translit": "َrَqَʿعَf", "root": "عقر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "فَكَيْفَ كَانَ عَذَابِى وَنُذُرِ ", "translation": "And how [severe] were My punishment and warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَكَيْفَ", "translit": "َfْyَkَf", "root": "كيف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَذَابِى", "translit": "àِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَنُذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُnَw", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "إِنَّآ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ صَيْحَةً وَٰحِدَةً فَكَانُوا۟ كَهَشِيمِ ٱلْمُحْتَظِرِ ", "translation": "Indeed, We sent upon them one shriek [i.e., blast from the sky], and they became like the dry twig fragments of an [animal] pen.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "صَيْحَةً", "translit": "ًẗَḥْyَṣ", "root": "صيح"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَٰحِدَةً", "translit": "ًẗَdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "فَكَانُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُnāَkَf", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَهَشِيمِ", "translit": "ِmyِhsَhَk", "root": "هشم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "ٱلْمُحْتَظِرِ", "translit": "ِrِẓَtْḥُmْlٱ", "root": "حظر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "وَلَقَدْ يَسَّرْنَا ٱلْقُرْءَانَ لِلذِّكْرِ فَهَلْ مِن مُّدَّكِرٍ ", "translation": "And We have certainly made the Qur’ān easy for remembrance, so is there any who will remember?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسَّرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَّsَy", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِلذِّكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِّhdlِl", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّدَّكِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِkَّdُّm", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "كَذَّبَتْ قَوْمُ لُوطٍۭ بِٱلنُّذُرِ ", "translation": "The people of Lot denied the warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَذَّبَتْ", "translit": "ْtَbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَq", "root": "قوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لُوطٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭṭwُl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلنُّذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُّnlٱِb", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "إِنَّآ أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ حَاصِبًا إِلَّآ ءَالَ لُوطٍۖ نَّجَّيْنَٰهُم بِسَحَرٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, We sent upon them a storm of stones, except the family of Lot - We saved them before dawn.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَرْسَلْنَا", "translit": "āَnْlَsْrَaʾ", "root": "رسل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "حَاصِبًا", "translit": "āًbِṣāَḥ", "root": "حصب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَّآ", "translit": "ٓāَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَالَ", "translit": "َlāَء", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لُوطٍ", "translit": "ٍṭwُl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "نَّجَّيْنَٰهُم", "translit": "mُhَٰnْyَّjgَّn", "root": "نجو"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بِسَحَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَḥَsِb", "root": "سحر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "نِّعْمَةً مِّنْ عِندِنَاۚ كَذَٰلِكَ نَجْزِى مَن شَكَرَ ", "translation": "As favor from Us. Thus do We reward he who is grateful.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "نِّعْمَةً", "translit": "ًẗَmْʿعِّn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عِندِنَا", "translit": "āَnِdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كَذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhdَk", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نَجْزِى", "translit": "àِzْjgَn", "root": "جزي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "شَكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَhs", "root": "شكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَنذَرَهُم بَطْشَتَنَا فَتَمَارَوْا۟ بِٱلنُّذُرِ ", "translation": "And he had already warned them of Our assault, but they disputed the warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَنذَرَهُم", "translit": "mُhَrَhdnَaʾ", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَطْشَتَنَا", "translit": "āَnَtَhsْṭَb", "root": "بطش"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَتَمَارَوْا۟", "translit": "۟āْwَrāَmَtَf", "root": "مري"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِٱلنُّذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُّnlٱِb", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "وَلَقَدْ رَٰوَدُوهُ عَن ضَيْفِهِۦ فَطَمَسْنَآ أَعْيُنَهُمْ فَذُوقُوا۟ عَذَابِى وَنُذُرِ ", "translation": "And they had demanded from him his guests, but We obliterated their eyes, [saying], \"Taste My punishment and warning.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "رَٰوَدُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُdَwَٰr", "root": "رود"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَن", "translit": "nَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ضَيْفِهِۦ", "translit": "ۦِhِfْyَḍ", "root": "ضيف"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَطَمَسْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْsَmَṭَf", "root": "طمس"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَعْيُنَهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَnُyْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَذُوقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqwُhdَf", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَذَابِى", "translit": "àِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "وَنُذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُnَw", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وَلَقَدْ صَبَّحَهُم بُكْرَةً عَذَابٌ مُّسْتَقِرٌّ ", "translation": "And there came upon them by morning an abiding punishment.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَبَّحَهُم", "translit": "mُhَḥَّbَṣ", "root": "صبح"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بُكْرَةً", "translit": "ًẗَrْkُb", "root": "بكر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَذَابٌ", "translit": "ٌbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّسْتَقِرٌّ", "translit": "ٌّrِqَtْsُّm", "root": "قرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "فَذُوقُوا۟ عَذَابِى وَنُذُرِ ", "translation": "So taste My punishment and warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَذُوقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqwُhdَf", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَذَابِى", "translit": "àِbāَhdَʿع", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَنُذُرِ", "translit": "ِrُhdُnَw", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "وَلَقَدْ يَسَّرْنَا ٱلْقُرْءَانَ لِلذِّكْرِ فَهَلْ مِن مُّدَّكِرٍ ", "translation": "And We have certainly made the Qur’ān easy for remembrance, so is there any who will remember?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسَّرْنَا", "translit": "āَnْrَّsَy", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقُرْءَانَ", "translit": "َnāَءْrُqْlٱ", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِلذِّكْرِ", "translit": "ِrْkِّhdlِl", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّدَّكِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِkَّdُّm", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "وَلَقَدْ جَآءَ ءَالَ فِرْعَوْنَ ٱلنُّذُرُ ", "translation": "And there certainly came to the people of Pharaoh warning.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjg", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ءَالَ", "translit": "َlāَء", "root": "اول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِرْعَوْنَ", "translit": "َnْwَʿعْrِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلنُّذُرُ", "translit": "ُrُhdُّnlٱ", "root": "نذر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "كَذَّبُوا۟ بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا كُلِّهَا فَأَخَذْنَٰهُمْ أَخْذَ عَزِيزٍ مُّقْتَدِرٍ ", "translation": "They denied Our signs, all of them, so We seized them with a seizure of one Exalted in Might and Perfect in Ability.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "كَذَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِـَٔايَٰتِنَا", "translit": "āَnِtَٰyāَٔـِb", "root": "ايي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "كُلِّهَا", "translit": "āَhِّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَأَخَذْنَٰهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhَٰnْhdَhkَaʾَf", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَخْذَ", "translit": "َhdْhkَaʾ", "root": "اخذ"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَزِيزٍ", "translit": "ٍzyِzَʿع", "root": "عزز"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّقْتَدِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِdَtْqُّm", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "أَكُفَّارُكُمْ خَيْرٌ مِّنْ أُو۟لَٰٓئِكُمْ أَمْ لَكُم بَرَآءَةٌ فِى ٱلزُّبُرِ ", "translation": "Are your disbelievers better than those [former ones], or have you immunity in the scriptures?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَكُفَّارُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُrāَّfُkَaʾ", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَيْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْyَhk", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "أُو۟لَٰٓئِكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkِyʾَٰٓl۟wُaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "لَكُم", "translit": "mُkَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بَرَآءَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَءٓāَrَb", "root": "برا"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلزُّبُرِ", "translit": "ِrُbُّzlٱ", "root": "زبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "أَمْ يَقُولُونَ نَحْنُ جَمِيعٌ مُّنتَصِرٌ ", "translation": "Or do they say, \"We are an assembly supporting [each other]\"?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَمْ", "translit": "ْmَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَمِيعٌ", "translit": "ٌʿعyِmَjg", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مُّنتَصِرٌ", "translit": "ٌrِṣَtnُّm", "root": "نصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "سَيُهْزَمُ ٱلْجَمْعُ وَيُوَلُّونَ ٱلدُّبُرَ ", "translation": "[Their] assembly will be defeated, and they will turn their backs [in retreat].[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "سَيُهْزَمُ", "translit": "ُmَzْhُyَs", "root": "هزم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَمْعُ", "translit": "ُʿعْmَjgْlٱ", "root": "جمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَيُوَلُّونَ", "translit": "َnwُّlَwُyَw", "root": "ولي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلدُّبُرَ", "translit": "َrُbُّdlٱ", "root": "دبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 46, "text": "بَلِ ٱلسَّاعَةُ مَوْعِدُهُمْ وَٱلسَّاعَةُ أَدْهَىٰ وَأَمَرُّ ", "translation": "But the Hour is their appointment [for due punishment], and the Hour is more disastrous and more bitter.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "بَلِ", "translit": "ِlَb", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلسَّاعَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَʿعāَّslٱ", "root": "سوع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَوْعِدُهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhُdِʿعْwَm", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلسَّاعَةُ", "translit": "ُẗَʿعāَّslٱَw", "root": "سوع"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَدْهَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhْdَaʾ", "root": "دهي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَأَمَرُّ", "translit": "ُّrَmَaʾَw", "root": "مرر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 47, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍ وَسُعُرٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, the criminals are in error and madness.[1].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُجْرِمِينَ", "translit": "َnyِmِrْjgُmْlٱ", "root": "جرم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍ", "translit": "ٍlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَسُعُرٍ", "translit": "ٍrُʿعُsَw", "root": "سعر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 48, "text": "يَوْمَ يُسْحَبُونَ فِى ٱلنَّارِ عَلَىٰ وُجُوهِهِمْ ذُوقُوا۟ مَسَّ سَقَرَ ", "translation": "The Day they are dragged into the Fire on their faces [it will be said], \"Taste the touch of Saqar.\"[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُسْحَبُونَ", "translit": "َnwُbَḥْsُy", "root": "سحب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلنَّارِ", "translit": "ِrāَّnlٱ", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وُجُوهِهِمْ", "translit": "ْmِhِhwُjgُw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ذُوقُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُqwُhd", "root": "ذوق"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مَسَّ", "translit": "َّsَm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "سَقَرَ", "translit": "َrَqَs", "root": ""}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 49, "text": "إِنَّا كُلَّ شَىْءٍ خَلَقْنَٰهُ بِقَدَرٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, all things We created with predestination.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَٰهُ", "translit": "ُhَٰnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بِقَدَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَdَqِb", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 50, "text": "وَمَآ أَمْرُنَآ إِلَّا وَٰحِدَةٌ كَلَمْحٍۭ بِٱلْبَصَرِ ", "translation": "And Our command is but one, like a glance of the eye.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَمْرُنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnُrْmَaʾ", "root": "امر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٰحِدَةٌ", "translit": "ٌẗَdِḥَٰw", "root": "وحد"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَلَمْحٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭḥْmَlَk", "root": "لمح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِٱلْبَصَرِ", "translit": "ِrَṣَbْlٱِb", "root": "بصر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 51, "text": "وَلَقَدْ أَهْلَكْنَآ أَشْيَاعَكُمْ فَهَلْ مِن مُّدَّكِرٍ ", "translation": "And We have already destroyed your kinds,[1] so is there any who will remember?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَآ", "translit": "ٓāَnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَشْيَاعَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَʿعāَyْhsَaʾ", "root": "شيع"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فَهَلْ", "translit": "ْlَhَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُّدَّكِرٍ", "translit": "ٍrِkَّdُّm", "root": "ذكر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 52, "text": "وَكُلُّ شَىْءٍ فَعَلُوهُ فِى ٱلزُّبُرِ ", "translation": "And everything they did is in written records.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "شَىْءٍ", "translit": "ٍءْàَhs", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَعَلُوهُ", "translit": "ُhwُlَʿعَf", "root": "فعل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلزُّبُرِ", "translit": "ِrُbُّzlٱ", "root": "زبر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 53, "text": "وَكُلُّ صَغِيرٍ وَكَبِيرٍ مُّسْتَطَرٌ ", "translation": "And every small and great [thing] is inscribed.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُkَw", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "صَغِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِغَṣ", "root": "صغر"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَكَبِيرٍ", "translit": "ٍryِbَkَw", "root": "كبر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُّسْتَطَرٌ", "translit": "ٌrَṭَtْsُّm", "root": "سطر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 54, "text": "إِنَّ ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ فِى جَنَّٰتٍ وَنَهَرٍ ", "translation": "Indeed, the righteous will be among gardens and rivers,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْمُتَّقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqَّtُmْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "جَنَّٰتٍ", "translit": "ٍtَّٰnَjg", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَنَهَرٍ", "translit": "ٍrَhَnَw", "root": "نهر"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 55, "text": "فِى مَقْعَدِ صِدْقٍ عِندَ مَلِيكٍ مُّقْتَدِرٍۭ ", "translation": "In a seat of honor near a Sovereign, Perfect in Ability.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَقْعَدِ", "translit": "ِdَʿعْqَm", "root": "قعد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "صِدْقٍ", "translit": "ٍqْdِṣ", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عِندَ", "translit": "َdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مَلِيكٍ", "translit": "ٍkyِlَm", "root": "ملك"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مُّقْتَدِرٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭrِdَtْqُّm", "root": "قدر"}], "hasIhya": true}], "tafsir": {"1": [{"arabic": "استمعوه وهم يلعبون لاهية قلوبهم ثم يعرفنا قرب القيامة فيقول اقتربت الساعة وانشق القمر إنهم يرونه بعيدا ونراه قريبا وما يدريك لعل الساعة تكون قريبا ثم يكون أحسن أحوالنا أن نتخذ دراسة هذا القرآن عملا فلا نتدبر معانيه ولا ننظر في كثرة أوصاف هذا اليوم وأساميه ولا نستعد للتخلص من دواهيه فنعوذ بالله من هذه الغفلة إن لم يداركنا الله بواسع رحمته", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "عليكم وميثاقه الذي واثقكم به ولقد يسرنا القرآن للذكر فهل من مدكر وتسمية هذا النمط تذكرا ليس ببعيد فكأن التذكر ضربان أحدهما أن يذكر صورة كانت حاضرة الوجود في قلبه لكن غابت بعد الوجود الآخر والآخر أن يذكر صورة كانت مضمنة فيه بالفطرة وهذه حقائق ظاهرة للناظر بنور البصيرة ثقيلة على من يستروجه (1) السماع والتقليد دون الكشف والعيان ولذلك تراه يتخبط في مثل هذه الآيات ويتعسف وفي تأويل التذكر بإقرار النفوس أنواعا من التعسفات ويتخايل إليه في الأخبار والآيات ضروب من المناقضات وربما يغلب ذلك عليه حتى ينظر إليها بعين ا", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "19": [{"arabic": "خليقته كما قال تعالى إنا أرسلنا عليهم ريحا صرصرا في يوم نحس مستمر تنزع الناس كأنهم أعجاز نخل منقعر ثم انظر إلى لطف الهواء ثم شدته وقوته مهما ضغط فى الماء فالزق المنفوخ يتحامل عليه الرجل القوى ليغمسه فى الماء فيعجز عنه والحديد الصلب تضعه على وجه الماء فيرسب فيه فانظر كيف ينقبض الهواء من الماء بقوته مع لطافته وبهذه الحكمة أمسك الله تعالى السفن على وجه الماء وكذلك كل مجوف فيه هواء لا يغوص فى الماء لأن الهواء ينقبض عن الغوص فى الماء فلا ينفصل عن السطح الداخل من السفينة فتبقى السفينة الثقيلة مع قوتها وصلابتها معلقة فى الهواء اللطيف كالذى يقع فى بئ", "english": "And among His signs is the subtle air (al-hawāʾ al-laṭīf) confined between the concavity of the heaven and the convexity of the earth. Its body is perceived by the sense of touch when the winds blow, yet its person and totality are not seen by the eyes. It is like a single sea, with birds soaring in the atmosphere of the sky, racing in swimming through it with their wings, just as the animals of the sea swim in water. Its sides and waves are agitated when the winds blow, just as the waves of the sea are agitated. So when God moves the air and makes it a blowing wind, if He wills, He makes it a bearer of glad tidings before His mercy — as He, glory be to Him, said: {«وَأَرْسَلْنَا الرِّيَاحَ لَوَاقِحَ»} (And We sent the winds as fertilizers) [Al-Hijr: 22] — and by its movement the spirit of the air reaches the animals and plants, and they become ready for growth. And if He wills, He makes it a chastisement upon the disobedient among His creatures — as He, exalted be He, said: {«إِنَّا أَرْسَلْنَا عَلَيْهِمْ رِيحًا صَرْصَرًا فِي يَوْمِ نَحْسٍ مُسْتَمِرٍّ * تَنْزِعُ النَّاسَ كَأَنَّهُمْ أَعْجَازُ نَخْلٍ مُنْقَعِرٍ»} (Indeed, We sent upon them a screaming wind in a day of continuous misfortune, tearing people away as if they were trunks of uprooted palm trees) [Al-Qamar: 19-20].", "book_title": "Contemplation", "section_title_ar": "Section 1276", "section_title_en": "The Discourse of the Nutfah (Sperm-Drop) and the Wonders of the Divine Penmanship, Air, and the Heavens", "section_index": 1276, "anchors": "- Root: نطف (n-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Pure, clear water; the seminal fluid; dripping or flowing gently.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): «النطفة: الماء الصافي، ويعبر بها عن ماء الرجل» — the sperm-drop as a designated reality (musammā) of human origination, a vehicle of divine creative power.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (haqīqah): clear water; metaphorical (majāz): the seminal drop as a locus of divine inscription.\n- Root: صور (ṣ-w-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To form, shape, or incline; the act of giving a figure.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): «الصورة: ما ينتقش به الأعيان» — sensible form (perceived by all) vs. intelligible form (perceived by the elite); here, the divine inscription of the embryo's features.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical curvature or inclination; metaphorical: the soul's inclination toward a thing — here, the soul's being drawn to the majesty of the Form-Giver.\n- Root: لطف (l-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Subtlety, gentleness, refinement; opposite of coarse or heavy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): «اللطيف إذا وصف به الجسم فضد الجثل» — subtlety as immaterial spiritual reality (laṭīfah), not corporeal substance (jawhar); divine attribute of penetrating knowledge and gentleness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: not coarse; metaphorical: «فلان لطيف يلطف لاستنباط المعاني» — subtle in extracting meanings; here, the air as a subtle divine reality (laṭīfah) bearing mercy or chastisement.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The permissibility of interposing elements between a governing word and its governed prepositional phrase when the intervening clause is complete in itself, as in «فبين الفاعلين من المباينة والتباعد ما بين الفعلين» — the separation of the two agents by a full clause mirrors the ontological distance between their acts.", "content_type": "dhikr", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on constant remembrance of God and contemplative invocations, from 'Contemplation'", "badge": "Dhikr"}], "22": [{"arabic": "عليكم وميثاقه الذي واثقكم به ولقد يسرنا القرآن للذكر فهل من مدكر وتسمية هذا النمط تذكرا ليس ببعيد فكأن التذكر ضربان أحدهما أن يذكر صورة كانت حاضرة الوجود في قلبه لكن غابت بعد الوجود الآخر والآخر أن يذكر صورة كانت مضمنة فيه بالفطرة وهذه حقائق ظاهرة للناظر بنور البصيرة ثقيلة على من يستروجه (1) السماع والتقليد دون الكشف والعيان ولذلك تراه يتخبط في مثل هذه الآيات ويتعسف وفي تأويل التذكر بإقرار النفوس أنواعا من التعسفات ويتخايل إليه في الأخبار والآيات ضروب من المناقضات وربما يغلب ذلك عليه حتى ينظر إليها بعين ا", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "24": [{"arabic": "لولا نزل هذا القرآن على رجل من القريتين عظيم} وقوله تعالى {أبشرا منا واحدا نتبعه} فهذه المعميات هي التي منعت الاهتداء والهداية الثانية وراء هذه الهداية العامة وهي التي يمد الله تعالى بها العبد حالا بعد حال وهي ثمرة المجاهدة حيث قال تعالى {والذين جاهدوا فينا لنهدينهم سبلنا} وهو المراد بقوله تعالى {والذين اهتدوا زادهم هدى} والهداية الثالثة وراء الثانية وهو النور الذي يشرق في عالم النبوة والولاية بعد كمال المجاهدة فيهتدي بها إلا ما لا يهتدى إليه بالعقل الذي يحصل به التكليف وإمكان تعلم العلوم وهو الهوى المطلق وما عداه حجاب", "english": "PageV04P107\nBut if one does not know what constitutes the welfare of his hereafter—so that he even supposes corruption to be welfare—then from where would mere volition benefit him? There is no benefit in volition, power, and causes except after guidance (hidāyah). For this reason, God—exalted is He—said: {«ربنا الذي أعطى كل شيء خلقه ثم هدى»} \"Our Lord who gave everything its creation, then guided.\" And He said—exalted is He—: {«ولولا فضل الله عليكم ورحمته ما زكى منكم أحد أبدا ولكن الله يزكي من يشاء»} \"And were it not for God's bounty upon you and His mercy, not one of you would ever be purified; but God purifies whom He wills.\" And the Prophet—peace be upon him—said: \"No one enters Paradise except by the mercy of God—exalted is He—,\" that is, by His guidance. So it was said: \"Not even you, O Messenger of God?\" He said: \"Not even I\" (1). And guidance (hidāyah) has three stations (manāzil):", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 999", "section_title_en": "The Degrees of Divine Guidance (Hidāyah), Righteous Discernment (Rushd), Straightening (Tasdīd), and Divine Support (Ta'yīd)", "section_index": 999, "anchors": "- Root: هدي (H-D-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of gentle, gracious direction (dalālah bi-luṭf); the root also yields \"gift\" (hadiyyah) and the leading beasts of prey (hawādī) that guide the herd.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Guidance is \"directional indication with gentleness\" (dalālah bi-luṭf), distinct from mere intellectual proof; it is a divine act that may be general (to all) or specific (to the elect), and its withholding is a form of abandonment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"He guided him to the path\" (hadāhu lil-sabīl). Metaphorical (majāz): \"He guided him\" meaning \"he preceded him\" (taqaddamahu), as a leader precedes the caravan; hence guidance is not merely pointing but active leading.\n- Root: رشد (R-SH-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The state of being rightly directed in one's affairs; integrity of judgment; names Rāshid, Rushayd derive from it.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Rushd is the opposite of ghayy (error/ruin); it is used like hidāyah but with a nuance of practical, executive discernment—knowing the good AND being moved to pursue it. Al-Raghib explicitly distinguishes the rushd of Abraham (given before, as a special gift) from the mere legal competence (rushd) of an orphan.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"A man is rāshid or rashīd\" (possessing rightness in conduct). Metaphorical: \"He is of sound lineage\" (huwa li-rushdah), i.e., legitimate—showing the root's extension to ontological soundness.\n- Root: سدد (S-D-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To aim correctly, to hit the mark; the arrow that flies straight (sadīd); the opposite of deviation (ighrāḍ).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (Not in the retrieved scholia, but by extension from the root's usage in the Qur'an, e.g., {wa-saddidū}, \"and be straight/aim rightly\")—it denotes the divine facilitation of the creature's movements so they align with the intended good.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He aimed the arrow and it went straight.\" Metaphorical: \"He was granted success in speech and action\" (sudda li-l-ṣawāb).\n- Root: أيد (A-Y-D)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Strength, support, reinforcement; the \"aid\" (ayd) of God is His empowering of His servant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): (By extension from {إذ أيدتك بروح القدس})—divine reinforcement through a holy spirit, i.e., an immaterial power that fortifies both inner perception (baṣīrah) and outer action.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"He strengthened him\" (ayyadahu). Metaphorical: Used for the conferral of spiritual power that makes the servant invincible against error.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Gradation and Specification (Tartīb wa-Takhṣīṣ) — The author structures a sequence of terms (hidāyah → rushd → tasdīd → ta'yīd) where each subsequent term is syntactically and semantically superordinate, building a hierarchy of divine acts. The particle \"wa-ammā\" (وأما) marks a new topic with contrastive focus, and the anaphoric references (هو، وهي) bind the exposition into a single periodic argument.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "32": [{"arabic": "عليكم وميثاقه الذي واثقكم به ولقد يسرنا القرآن للذكر فهل من مدكر وتسمية هذا النمط تذكرا ليس ببعيد فكأن التذكر ضربان أحدهما أن يذكر صورة كانت حاضرة الوجود في قلبه لكن غابت بعد الوجود الآخر والآخر أن يذكر صورة كانت مضمنة فيه بالفطرة وهذه حقائق ظاهرة للناظر بنور البصيرة ثقيلة على من يستروجه (1) السماع والتقليد دون الكشف والعيان ولذلك تراه يتخبط في مثل هذه الآيات ويتعسف وفي تأويل التذكر بإقرار النفوس أنواعا من التعسفات ويتخايل إليه في الأخبار والآيات ضروب من المناقضات وربما يغلب ذلك عليه حتى ينظر إليها بعين ا", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "40": [{"arabic": "عليكم وميثاقه الذي واثقكم به ولقد يسرنا القرآن للذكر فهل من مدكر وتسمية هذا النمط تذكرا ليس ببعيد فكأن التذكر ضربان أحدهما أن يذكر صورة كانت حاضرة الوجود في قلبه لكن غابت بعد الوجود الآخر والآخر أن يذكر صورة كانت مضمنة فيه بالفطرة وهذه حقائق ظاهرة للناظر بنور البصيرة ثقيلة على من يستروجه (1) السماع والتقليد دون الكشف والعيان ولذلك تراه يتخبط في مثل هذه الآيات ويتعسف وفي تأويل التذكر بإقرار النفوس أنواعا من التعسفات ويتخايل إليه في الأخبار والآيات ضروب من المناقضات وربما يغلب ذلك عليه حتى ينظر إليها بعين ا", "english": "I say: Know that the washing of the eye is forbidden — that is, the eye of insight (ʿayn al-baṣīrah) is not to be washed with the water of blind imitation and mere hearsay, for it is the organ by which the realities of the intellect are disclosed. And the divisions of what the name \"intellect\" (al-ʿaql) is applied to have become clear: it is said of the inborn faculty (al-gharīzah), and it is said of the knowledge that is acquired through that faculty, and it is said of the state of that knowledge when it governs the soul, and it is said of the very actions that proceed from that governance.", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 68", "section_title_en": "The Washing of the Eye is Forbidden — On the True Meaning of Intellect (ʿAql) and Its Divisions", "section_index": 68, "anchors": "- Root: عقل (ʿAql)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes binding, restraining, and holding back — the intellect is that which restrains the soul from base desires and prevents it from straying.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العقل يقال للقوة المتهيئة لقبول العلم، ويقال للعلم الذي يستفيده الإنسان بتلك القوة عقل\" — al-ʿaql is said of the faculty prepared to receive knowledge, and it is also said of the knowledge acquired by that faculty. It is the instrument of divine discernment and the locus of taklīf (moral responsibility).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): the inborn faculty of discernment. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"نخلة لا تعقل الإبار إذا لم تقبله\" — a palm tree that does not accept pollination is said to be \"not to ʿaqil\" the pollen, extending the root to the sense of \"accepting/receiving.\" Thus, knowledge is called ʿaql metaphorically as its fruit.\n- Root: بصر (Baṣar)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Sight, perception — both of the eye and of the heart.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"البصر يقال للجارحة الناظرة... ويقال لقوة القلب المدركة: بصيرة وبصر\" — baṣar is said of the seeing organ, and of the perceiving power of the heart: baṣīrah and baṣar. The inner sight (baṣīrah) perceives immaterial realities.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: ocular vision. Metaphorical: \"هذه آية مبصرة\" — this is an illuminating sign; \"واجعلني بصيرة عليهم أي رقيبا وشاهدا\" — make me a watcher over them. The inner eye perceives what the outer eye cannot.\n- Root: فطر (Faṭara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To split, cleave, originate, create — the primordial act of divine creation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الفطر: الشق طولا... وفطر الله الخلق\" — the root is splitting lengthwise; God's \"fatr\" of creation is His originating of things. \"فطرة الله التي فطر الناس عليها\" — the primordial disposition upon which God originated mankind.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"فطر الله الخلق، وهو فاطر السموات: مبتدعها\" — God originated creation; He is the Originator of the heavens. \"وكل مولود يولد على الفطرة\" — every newborn is born upon the fiṭrah, i.e., the innate disposition receptive to the religion of truth.\n- Root: نفس (Nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Self, soul, spirit; the innermost reality of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: تعلم ما في نفسي ولا أعلم ما في نفسك\" — nafs is the spirit/soul; \"You know what is in my nafs and I know not what is in Your nafs\" — the nafs is the seat of inner assent and recognition.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the self. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — he poured out his nafs, i.e., his blood. The nafs is the locus of the primordial covenant (mīthāq).\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The construction \"إنما العاقل من آمن بالله\" restricts the predicate: the intellect is not merely a faculty of cleverness but is defined by its fruit — faith and God-consciousness (taqwā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "48": [{"arabic": "نار جهنم دعا ويوم يسحبون فى النار على وجوههم ويوم تقلب وجوههم فى النار ويوم لا يجزى والد عن ولده ويوم يفر المرء من أخيه وأمه وأبيه ويوم لا ينطقون ولا يؤذن لهم فيعتذرون يوم لا مرد له من الله يوم هم بارزون ويوم هم على النار يفتنون يوم لا ينفع مال ولا بنون يوم لا ينفع الظالمين معذرتهم ولهم اللعنة ولهم سوء الدار يوم ترد فيه المعاذير وتبلى السرائر وتظهر الضمائر وتكشف الأستار يوم تخشع فيه الأبصار وتسكن الأصوات ويقل فيه الالتفات وتبرز الخفيات وتظهر الخطيئات يوم يساق العباد ومعهم الأشهاد ويشيب ال", "english": "So prepare yourself, O wretched one, for this Day of immense magnitude, of prolonged duration, of overwhelming dominion, whose appointed time is near — a Day when you shall see the heaven split asunder, and the stars scattered in its terror, and the shining planets extinguished, and the sun folded up, and the mountains set in motion, and the pregnant camels neglected, and the wild beasts gathered together, and the seas set ablaze, and the souls paired with their bodies, and the Hellfire ignited, and Paradise brought near, and the mountains pulverized, and the earth stretched out — a Day when you shall see the earth shaken with its quaking, and the earth casts forth its burdens. On that Day shall people issue forth in scattered groups to be shown their deeds — a Day when the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with a single crushing. Then on that Day the Imminent Event shall come to pass, and the heaven shall split open, for on that Day it shall be frail, and the angels shall be at its edges, and eight shall bear the Throne of your Lord above them on that Day. On that Day you shall be presented — no secret of yours shall remain hidden. A Day when the mountains shall move and you shall see the earth laid bare — a Day when the earth shall be shaken with a violent shaking, and the mountains crumbled to dust, becoming scattered particles — a Day when people shall be like scattered moths, and the mountains like carded wool — a Day when every nursing mother shall forget what she nursed, and every pregnant female shall cast down her burden, and you shall see people drunk, yet they are not drunk, but the chastisement of God is severe — a Day when the earth shall be changed to other than this earth, and the heavens, and they shall appear before God, the One, the All-Compelling — a Day when the mountains shall be blown away as dust, leaving a level, bare plain, wherein you see no crookedness nor curve — a Day when you see the mountains and suppose them solid, yet they pass by like the passing of clouds — a Day when the heaven shall split open and become rosy like molten oil. Then on that Day no human nor jinn shall be asked about his sin. A Day when the disobedient shall be prevented from speech, and shall not be asked about their crimes, but shall be seized by their forelocks and their feet — a Day when every soul shall find what it did of good brought forth present, and what it did of evil, wishing that between it and that evil there were a far distance — a Day when every soul shall know what it has sent ahead, and shall witness what it has forwarded and what it has left behind — a Day when tongues shall be struck mute and the bodily members shall speak — a Day whose mention turned the hair of the Master of the Messengers white, when al-Ṣiddīq (may God be pleased with him) said to him: \"I see that you have turned white-haired, O Messenger of God.\" He said: \"Sūrat Hūd and its sisters have made me white-haired\" — namely, al-Wāqiʿah, al-Mursalāt, ʿAmma Yatasāʾalūn, and Idhā al-Shamsu Kuwwirat.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1339", "section_title_en": "The Names and Attributes of the Day of Resurrection — An Exhortation to the Negligent", "section_index": 1339, "anchors": "- Root: يوم (yawm)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"اليوم: وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها، وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — the day as a temporal span, extended metaphorically to any epoch or era.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"اليوم يعبر به عن وقت طلوع الشمس إلى غروبها. وقد يعبر به عن مدة من الزمان أي مدة كانت\" — theological usage denotes the appointed time of reckoning, magnified by divine attribution (أيام الله).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): a measured solar period. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"ذكر في أيام العرب كذا أي في وقائعها\" — days as epochal events; thus \"the Day\" is the supreme epochal event of divine justice.\n- Root: نفس (nafs)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: النفس: الروح والذات — the self, soul, or very essence.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح... وقوله: ويحذركم الله نفسه فنفسه: ذاته\" — nafs as the innermost reality of a being; in the eschatological context, souls are \"paired\" (زوجت) with bodies for judgment.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the soul as life-principle. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — nafs as the total self, accountable in its entirety.\n- Root: ارض (arḍ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء — the earth as the corporeal substratum beneath the heavens.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأرض: الجرم المقابل للسماء... ويعبر بها عن أسفل الشيء\" — the earth as the lowest register of creation, subject to total transformation on that Day.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the physical earth. Metaphorical: \"تأرض فلان: لزم الأرض\" — to cling low; on that Day the earth itself is leveled, flattened, and replaced.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The repeated \"يوم\" clauses function as temporal restrictors (ẓurūf) binding each predicate to that single, all-encompassing moment; the accumulation of names (أسامي) serves not as mere repetition but as focused predication upon the multifarious realities of a single event.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "53": [{"arabic": "واجب الحصول بعد سبق المشيئة فلا راد لحكمه ولا معقب لقضائه وأمره بل كل صغير وكبير مستطر وحصوله بقدر معلوم منتظر وما أصابك لم يكن ليخطئك وما أخطأك لم يكن ليصيبك ولنقتصر على هذه المرامز من علوم المكاشفة التي هي أصول مقام التوكل ولنرجع إلى علم المعاملة إن شاء الله تعالى وحسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل", "english": "...the killing — rather, He stated explicitly, and He, exalted, said: {فَلَمْ تَقْتُلُوهُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ قَتَلَهُمْ} (\"You did not kill them, but God killed them\") and He, exalted, said: {وَمَا رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ رَمَىٰ} (\"You did not throw when you threw, but God threw\"). This is a combination of negation and affirmation in appearance, but its meaning is: \"You did not throw\" in the sense by which the Lord is a thrower, \"when you threw\" in the sense by which the servant is a thrower — for the two are two distinct meanings.", "book_title": "Monotheism and Reliance", "section_title_ar": "Section 1115", "section_title_en": "On the Unification of Divine Agency and the Foundation of Trust (Tawḥīd as the Root of Tawakkul)", "section_index": 1115, "anchors": "- Root: ق-ت-ل (Q-T-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning: \"to kill, to slay\" — the act of causing death by direct agency.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In the Qur'anic context {فَلَمْ تَقْتُلُوهُمْ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ قَتَلَهُمْ} (8:17), the verb is negated for the creature and affirmed for the Creator, indicating that true creative agency (ījād) belongs to God alone, while the creature's act is merely instrumental and attributive.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) usage assigns the verb to the true originator; metaphorical (majāz) usage assigns it to the proximate instrument, as in \"the king killed\" when the executioner strikes.\n- Root: ر-م-ي (R-M-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To throw, to cast\" — physical projection of an object.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): In {وَمَا رَمَيْتَ إِذْ رَمَيْتَ وَلَٰكِنَّ اللَّهَ رَمَىٰ} (8:17), the dual affirmation and negation show two distinct meanings of \"throwing\": the human act (kasb) versus the divine act (khalq).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): The literal sense belongs to the agent who originates the act ex nihilo; the metaphorical sense belongs to the one through whom the act occurs as a vessel.\n- Root: ع-ل-م (ʿ-L-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"Knowledge, perception, cognition\" — the apprehension of a thing as it truly is.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Knowledge is of two kinds: (1) apprehension of the essence of a thing, and (2) judgment about a thing's existence or non-existence of attributes. Divine knowledge (ʿilm) is the source of all teaching, as in {عَلَّمَ بِالْقَلَمِ} (96:4).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to know\" as direct cognition; also \"to teach\" (ʿallama) as the transmission of that cognition.\n- Root: و-ك-ل (W-K-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"To entrust, to delegate, to rely upon\" — the act of appointing another as deputy and placing one's affair in their hands.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Tawakkul is to rely upon another and make them one's agent. The Wakīl (Trustee) is He who undertakes the affair of His servant, as in {وَكَفَىٰ بِاللَّهِ وَكِيلًا} (4:81).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"to entrust an affair to someone.\" Metaphorical: \"to rely upon\" in the sense of inner confidence and tranquility of heart.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The particle \"innamā\" restricts the predicate to the subject alone, excluding all others. Also applicable: the rule of negation and affirmation (nafy wa-ithbāt) in a single sentence, where the negation applies to one sense and the affirmation to another, requiring semantic disambiguation (taṣrīf al-maʿnā).", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monotheism and Reliance'", "badge": "Insight"}], "54": [{"arabic": "كأمثال اللؤلؤ المكنون جزاء بما كانوا يعملون في مقام أمين في جنات وعيون في جنات ونهر في مقعد صدق عند مليك مقتدر ينظرون فيها إلى وجه الملك الكريم وقد أشرقت في وجوههم نضرة النعيم لا يرهقهم فتر ولا ذلة بل عباد مكرمون وبأنواع التحف من ربهم يتعاهدون فهم فيما اشتهيت أنفسهم خالدون لا يخافون فيها ولا يحزنون وهم من ريب المنون آمنون فهم فيها يتنعمون ويأكلون من أطعمتها ويشربون من أنهارها لبنا وخمرا وعسلا في أنهار أراضيها من فضة وحصباؤها مرجان وعلى أرض ترابها مسك أذفر ونباتها زعفران ويمطرون من سحاب فيها من ماء النسرين على كثبان الكافور ويؤتون بأكواب وأي أكواب بأك", "english": "For God Most High has said: {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم وإن الفجار لفي جحيم»} — \"Indeed the righteous are in bliss, and indeed the wicked are in Hellfire.\" So present yourself before these two verses, and you will have known your resting-place between the two abodes. And God knows best.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1351", "section_title_en": "Section 1351 — On the Description of Paradise and the Varieties of Its Bliss", "section_index": 1351, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes softness, ease, and pleasant living; al-Ayn notes it among names of the leopard and tiger, while Lisan records \"Nuʿm\" as a proper name.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — niʿmah is the excellent state/condition one inhabits; niʿīm is the experience of delight. The verse {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم»} designates the abiding state of bliss as a settled condition, not a transient act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"في النعمة والنعيم\" — dwelling in bounty and bliss. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"خفت نعامتهم: ذهبوا\" — ostriches departing; here the root's literal sense of softness is transfigured into the theological state of perpetual ease, free from all harshness.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: face, front, direction; al-Ayn cites \"وجه الصواب\" — the face/course of correctness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the noblest part of the outward body, hence used for the essence (al-dhāt) of a thing; in {«ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام»} it signifies the Divine Essence. Here \"وجه الملك الكريم\" — the Face of the Generous King — denotes the Beatific Vision of God's Essence, not a corporeal organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical face. Metaphorical: \"وجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" — the foremost, noblest of a people; thus the \"face\" of the King is His most august presence.\n- Root: مرج (m-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: mixing, confusion, letting loose; al-Ayn: \"المرج: الشهد\" — also pure honey; Lisan: \"مرج أمرهم: اختلط\" — their affair became disordered.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل المرج: الخلط\" — mixing; \"أمر مريج\" — a confused affair (Q. 50:5). In this text, the rivers of Paradise \"مطردة\" (flowing) are not \"ممرجة\" (mixed/confused) — rather they run pure and distinct, each in its channel.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"مرج الدواب: أرسلها في المرج\" — releasing beasts into pasture. Metaphorical: \"مرج الله البحرين\" — God let the two seas flow freely; \"مرجت عهودهم\" — covenants became disordered. The contrast sharpens the purity of Paradise's rivers.\n- Root: امن (a-m-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: safety, freedom from fear; al-Ayn treats it under the hamzah chapter as the state of security.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الأمن: طمأنينة النفس وزوال الخوف\" — tranquility of the soul and removal of fear. In the Hereafter, \"آمنون\" is not mere absence of threat but the positive, settled peace of the soul in the Divine presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أمنته وآمننيه\" — to grant safety. Metaphorical: \"فرس أمين القوى\" — a horse whose strength is trustworthy; here the blessed are \"آمنون من ريب المنون\" — secure from the vicissitudes of fate, a security that is trustworthy and unbreachable.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule permits separation between a preposition and its object by an element upon which speech may properly pause, as in \"إن به لضرًا\" — here \"فهم فيما اشتهت أنفسهم خالدون\" (in what their souls desire, abiding eternally) interposes the clause of desire between the prepositional phrase and its predicate, heightening rhetorical emphasis on the soul's fulfillment.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "55": [{"arabic": "في جنات ونهر في مقعد صدق عند مليك مقتدر ينظرون فيها إلى وجه الملك الكريم وقد أشرقت في وجوههم نضرة النعيم لا يرهقهم فتر ولا ذلة بل عباد مكرمون وبأنواع التحف من ربهم يتعاهدون فهم فيما اشتهيت أنفسهم خالدون لا يخافون فيها ولا يحزنون وهم من ريب المنون آمنون فهم فيها يتنعمون ويأكلون من أطعمتها ويشربون من أنهارها لبنا وخمرا وعسلا في أنهار أراضيها من فضة وحصباؤها مرجان وعلى أرض ترابها مسك أذفر ونباتها زعفران ويمطرون من سحاب فيها من ماء النسرين على كثبان الكافور ويؤتون بأكواب وأي أكواب بأك", "english": "For God Most High has said: {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم وإن الفجار لفي جحيم»} — \"Indeed the righteous are in bliss, and indeed the wicked are in Hellfire.\" So present yourself before these two verses, and you will have known your resting-place between the two abodes. And God knows best.", "book_title": "Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment", "section_title_ar": "Section 1351", "section_title_en": "Section 1351 — On the Description of Paradise and the Varieties of Its Bliss", "section_index": 1351, "anchors": "- Root: نعم (n-ʿ-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root denotes softness, ease, and pleasant living; al-Ayn notes it among names of the leopard and tiger, while Lisan records \"Nuʿm\" as a proper name.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النعمة: الحالة الحسنة\" — niʿmah is the excellent state/condition one inhabits; niʿīm is the experience of delight. The verse {«إن الأبرار لفي نعيم»} designates the abiding state of bliss as a settled condition, not a transient act.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"في النعمة والنعيم\" — dwelling in bounty and bliss. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"خفت نعامتهم: ذهبوا\" — ostriches departing; here the root's literal sense of softness is transfigured into the theological state of perpetual ease, free from all harshness.\n- Root: وجه (w-j-h)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: face, front, direction; al-Ayn cites \"وجه الصواب\" — the face/course of correctness.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The face is the noblest part of the outward body, hence used for the essence (al-dhāt) of a thing; in {«ويبقى وجه ربك ذو الجلال والإكرام»} it signifies the Divine Essence. Here \"وجه الملك الكريم\" — the Face of the Generous King — denotes the Beatific Vision of God's Essence, not a corporeal organ.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical face. Metaphorical: \"وجه القوم، وهؤلاء وجوه البلد\" — the foremost, noblest of a people; thus the \"face\" of the King is His most august presence.\n- Root: مرج (m-r-j)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: mixing, confusion, letting loose; al-Ayn: \"المرج: الشهد\" — also pure honey; Lisan: \"مرج أمرهم: اختلط\" — their affair became disordered.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل المرج: الخلط\" — mixing; \"أمر مريج\" — a confused affair (Q. 50:5). In this text, the rivers of Paradise \"مطردة\" (flowing) are not \"ممرجة\" (mixed/confused) — rather they run pure and distinct, each in its channel.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"مرج الدواب: أرسلها في المرج\" — releasing beasts into pasture. Metaphorical: \"مرج الله البحرين\" — God let the two seas flow freely; \"مرجت عهودهم\" — covenants became disordered. The contrast sharpens the purity of Paradise's rivers.\n- Root: امن (a-m-n)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core root: safety, freedom from fear; al-Ayn treats it under the hamzah chapter as the state of security.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل الأمن: طمأنينة النفس وزوال الخوف\" — tranquility of the soul and removal of fear. In the Hereafter, \"آمنون\" is not mere absence of threat but the positive, settled peace of the soul in the Divine presence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"أمنته وآمننيه\" — to grant safety. Metaphorical: \"فرس أمين القوى\" — a horse whose strength is trustworthy; here the blessed are \"آمنون من ريب المنون\" — secure from the vicissitudes of fate, a security that is trustworthy and unbreachable.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The rule permits separation between a preposition and its object by an element upon which speech may properly pause, as in \"إن به لضرًا\" — here \"فهم فيما اشتهت أنفسهم خالدون\" (in what their souls desire, abiding eternally) interposes the clause of desire between the prepositional phrase and its predicate, heightening rhetorical emphasis on the soul's fulfillment.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Love, Longing, Intimacy and Contentment'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 200, 1441], [2, 1441, 3126], [3, 3126, 4606], [4, 4606, 5560]], "54:1": [[1, 200, 1441], [2, 1441, 3126], [3, 3126, 4606], [4, 4606, 5560]], "2": [[1, 200, 923], [2, 923, 2132], [3, 2132, 3184], [4, 3184, 4912], [5, 4912, 7190], [6, 7190, 7693], [7, 7693, 8800]], "54:2": [[1, 200, 923], [2, 923, 2132], [3, 2132, 3184], [4, 3184, 4912], [5, 4912, 7190], [6, 7190, 7693], [7, 7693, 8800]], "3": [[1, 810, 2846], [2, 2846, 5813], [3, 5813, 8540], [4, 8540, 9458], [5, 9458, 1002